Z||000006||theogre||10-12-2000||02:16 PM||||I've pulled this back up because there's an important update. (It will get added to the cave soon.)<P>As some of you may remember I tried installing a set of Accell coils on my car and had trouble with them. I hate being stuck with prarts I can't use so I decided to see if I could figure out just what the problem is. I went looking for a used OE GM PIM to see it maybe the one that's on there now was batty. (I took the whole brick... I'm going to get another just for extras.)<P>while pulling off the used ignition parts at the scrap yard I found that in between the coils and PIM is a stamped galvanized steel sheild. A part that my own brick doesn't have.<P>My brick had been serviced with a PIM from Standard. When the service was done the old shield was likely pitched with the OE PIM.<P>At first glance you'd thing the sheild was part of the PIM. Since it is stuck on tight enough to stay in place by itself that first impression gets reinforced. (I'd never thought about it before. DIS PIM's hardly ever fail. I've never had to replace one before. You wouldn't mess with the shield to change coils.)<P>The fact is the sheild IS NOT part of the PIM and needs to be transfered to any new PIM installed in the brick.<P>The shield does 2 things. It provides magnetic shielding for the PIM. The coils build and break rather dense magnetic fields and can generate stray signals in surounding parts.<P>I've also found the shield provides thermal protection for the coils. Previously my coils would get quite hot. Now even tho the PIM gets hot as always the coils don't even get warm. You wouldn't think a metal plate would do that...<P>So now I've got the OE PIM and the Accell coils on without any problem so far. The coils don't even get warm. ((Heat doesn't always wreck the coils but it will change how the coils work. They loose allot of efficiency as they heat up.))<P>it's posible part of my problem may be the aftermarket PIM, but I suspect it was the missing shield more. The Accell coils got pretty warm without the shield. At some point I'll try my old PIM again just to make sure that wasn't the cause. (I had already tried another aftermarket PIM and it would not work right with the accell coils either.)<P>I'm thinking now that the Accell coils just can't take the heat as well as the OE coil set can. (Something that agrees with other people that have noted the same about Accell's HEI coil.)<P>Would I buy the Accell coils again? probably not. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||10-12-2000||05:09 PM||||Damn I was hoping someone was working on a way to put DIS ona V6 and almost had it figured out.  Oh well.<P>Actually I was thinking what is keeping us from just going and grabbing a computer and wiring harness from a Cavalier 3.1/2.8 with DIS?  I mean the Cavalier's had a pretty radical cam like ours and a good prom program.  Either way a custom Chip can be made to replace the Cavalier one if one wants.  Westers Garage made a custom prom for my fiero with nitrous and my bolt on mods.  He could do one for a DIS computer I am sure too.  What do you V6 guys think?<P>Oh yea Ogre, good write up here.  I don't want to take away from what you had originally posted.  Good for all 87-88 4cyl owners to know about.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-12-2000||07:47 PM||||To add DIS to a motor that doesn't have it you need more than the computer. allot of the sensors are the same but you need to figure out how to add the notchwheel and crank sensor.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sarlacc||10-12-2000||08:06 PM||||Or just use a 3100/3400 block, they have the nice hole in the side where a crank sensor mounts.  The computer is not a problem, the DIS ignition module generates the same reference pulse that the computer would see with a distributor system.<p>[This message has been edited by Sarlacc (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||TheFiero64||10-13-2000||10:55 AM||||When I still had my iron duke in my 88 I had the accell coils. It seemed to run great with them. I just had to cut the little plastic tabs off the back. I like the yellow and the Lethal voltage stickers on them.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000011||Shaun41178||10-13-2000||01:21 PM||||The reason I am interested in this with a V6 is because I have a Aluminum racing block and it comes with a provision for a crank sensor and I have a 3.1 crank with the disk on it with the notches for a signal for the crank sensor.  <P>Sarlacc: you said the DIS system produces the same reference signal to the ecm that the Distributor does.  However would you still be able to use the Fiero computer?  I don't quite understand how the Fiero computer could translate the signal from the crank sensor if it wasn't programed to or have the wiring provisions for it.  Or does it not matter if it was programed for it or not?  Or does one of the ignition wires from the stock distributor ignition module to the ignition coil get transfered down to the crank sensor?  Then does the other ignition wires that would come from the ignition module to the coil get transfered to the DIS coils?  This is the only part I am unsure of.  If someone knew how to route the wires from the computer to the ignition module to the coil and reroute them to the crank sensor and DIS coils and still use the Fiero computer then I would be all game for that.  <P>Can anyone else help me out?  I find this interesting.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shaun41178||10-15-2000||01:29 PM||||Anyone  out there that can give me any info on swapping DIS into a V6 Fiero as far as the wiring is concerned?  Or even just pointing me in the proper direction of where to find the info at would be great too.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||10-15-2000||03:12 PM||||Find a GM car that had a V6 with DIS and get hold of the shop book or look it up in ALLdata. That would give you what you need.<P><BR>Back to the topic,,,<P>The fact that the 120+ US dollar PIM has to be altered to accept the Accell DIS coil is one of the other reasons I will not recomend them in future. I think its completely moronic that Accel couldn't be bothered to propperly fit the coils to the PIM. Those 2 ears are what keep the metal shield in place on the PIM.<P>I also don't like the small black piece that is suposed to seal the terminals. It is just stuck in. What little glue was put to hold it just popped loose the first time I lifted the coil off the PIM. That piece is suposed to provide the weather seal for the primary terminals. (I packed my coils with Dielectric while the plastic thing was off. They ship dry.)<P>While they look nice in Accell yellow I don't think at all the product is well made. I think this is probably one of Accell's worst product development efforts.<P>I altered the coils to fit the PIM instead of altering the PIM. This gives me a top and bottom keyed coil. Now if I take them off with the plug wires on I simply can't put them back wrong. (You could do that to OE coils by just filling one guide hole with glue and let it dry.)||||reg||1
